{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790263992,"nonce":"p0s73QavSEG1o+f0JMV7MchY0ws=","title":"scimark SOR: every G[x, y] goes to Array2D.__getitem__ with an Object key","message":"`SOR(args)` makes its grid with `n, cycles, Array = map(eval, args)`, so `a` and `G` in `SOR_execute` are Object and every `G[x, y]` is `zb_any_getitem` with a boxed tuple, reaching `Array2D.__getitem__(self, x_y: Object)` through the instance hook; `(x, y) = x_y` then hands `_idx` two Objects. There is no typed call site of `Array2D.__getitem__` in the SOR path at all, and `ArrayList` overrides `__getitem__`/`__setitem__`, so per-signature instances of closed items (which only a typed site of a method nothing overrides reaches) cannot help here: ZYNTAX_TRACE_TYPES shows `Array2D$__getitem__(self: Class(19), x_y: Object)` and `Array2D$_idx(self, x: Object, y: Object)` with no instance.\n\nWhat would: an instance reached from the dynamic edge. The `__getitem__` hook could test the key's box (a tuple of two ints) and call a `Tuple(Int, Int)` instance, made for the key shapes the method's own body unpacks, falling back to the generic function otherwise; `_idx` then gets an `(Int, Int)` instance from that body's typed call. Instances for methods a subclass overrides need a per-signature dispatcher as well.","files":null}]}